select 'bbbb0005-0000-4000-8000-000000000029', v.pos, v.title, v.kind, v.body::jsonb, v.notes, v.mins
from (values
  (1, 'Diagnostic: where are you now?', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"The study of the aims, methods, and institutions of teaching and learning is called...","options":["economics","pedagogy and the foundations of education","astronomy","accounting"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Pedagogy is the art and science of teaching; the foundations course surveys its aims, history, and methods."},{"prompt":"A lesson objective written as ''students will be able to...'' is most useful because it...","options":["hides the goal from learners","states an observable, assessable outcome","replaces all assessment","is required by law everywhere"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Good objectives name something you can observe and measure, which lets you align teaching and assessment."},{"prompt":"Assessment that happens during learning to guide the next teaching step is called...","options":["summative","formative","terminal","ceremonial"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Formative assessment is low-stakes and happens in progress; summative measures learning at the end."},{"prompt":"A teaching philosophy centered on timeless great works and universal truths is...","options":["progressivism","perennialism","behaviorism","unschooling"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Perennialism holds that certain ideas are enduring, so students study classic texts and disciplined reasoning."},{"prompt":"The idea that learners build new understanding on top of what they already know is central to...","options":["constructivism","rote drill","standardized ranking","seat time"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Constructivism says knowledge is actively built through experience and reflection, not simply received."},{"prompt":"Bloom''s taxonomy is best described as...","options":["a grading scale from A to F","a ladder of thinking skills from remembering to creating","a seating chart","a school funding formula"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Bloom''s taxonomy ranks cognitive tasks from remember and understand up to evaluate and create."},{"prompt":"Ensuring every learner, regardless of background, can access and succeed in school is the goal of...","options":["tracking by wealth","equity and inclusion","tuition increases","reduced library hours"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Equity gives each learner what they need to succeed; inclusion teaches diverse learners together with support."},{"prompt":"A teacher who regularly examines and adjusts their own practice is engaged in...","options":["reflective practice","passive observation","standardized testing","payroll"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Reflective practice turns experience into expertise by reviewing evidence of teaching and adjusting."}]}'::jsonb, null, 10),
  (2, 'What education is and its purposes', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Define education as a deliberate process of fostering learning, and explain the main purposes schools are asked to serve. Warm-up: from the diagnostic, name one purpose of education beyond passing on facts. By the end you will be able to distinguish the personal, economic, civic, and cultural aims of schooling, and say why these aims sometimes pull against one another. You will also be able to tell the difference between education, which is broad and lifelong, and schooling, which is the formal institution we build to deliver part of it. This matters because almost every argument about schools - what to teach, how long, and for whom - is really an argument about which purpose should come first. Naming the purposes clearly is the first tool of a thoughtful teacher."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Education is the deliberate cultivation of knowledge, skills, character, and habits. It happens formally in schools, informally at home and in communities, and non-formally in clubs, workplaces, and courses. Societies fund schooling because they ask it to do several jobs at once. The personal aim is individual growth: literacy, numeracy, curiosity, and the capacity to think for oneself. The economic aim is preparation for work and a productive economy. The civic aim is forming citizens who can take part in public life. The cultural aim is passing on language, values, and shared knowledge from one generation to the next. A fifth aim, often stated today, is equity: using schools to widen opportunity rather than simply reproduce existing advantage. These purposes overlap but can conflict. Preparing a narrow workforce quickly may crowd out broad personal growth; emphasizing one culture may sideline others. Good teachers hold these aims in mind and make deliberate choices about which they serve in any given lesson."},{"heading":"Key thinkers & examples","text":"The word education traces to Latin roots meaning to bring up and to lead out, hinting at both nurture and drawing out potential. Plato, in the Republic, argued that schooling should form just citizens for a just society, linking education to civic purpose. John Dewey insisted that education is not mere preparation for life but a form of living itself, stressing growth and democratic participation. Economists such as Theodore Schultz described schooling as human capital, an investment that raises future productivity. A modern example of competing aims: a state may add computer science to the timetable for economic reasons, while parents press for more history and arts for cultural and personal reasons, and the ministry defends civics for democratic reasons. Each group is appealing to a real purpose of education; the disagreement is about emphasis, not about whether education matters at all."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Name the four classic purposes of education discussed here. 2) Is a weekend chess club formal, non-formal, or informal education? 3) Give one way the economic aim can conflict with the personal aim. 4) What is the difference between education and schooling? 5) Which aim is invoked when a school teaches voting and the rule of law? Answers: 1) personal growth, economic preparation, civic formation, and cultural transmission - equity is a common fifth. 2) non-formal, since it is organized but sits outside the official school system. 3) narrowing the timetable to job skills can squeeze out broad personal development such as the arts. 4) education is the broad lifelong process of learning; schooling is the formal institution built to deliver part of it. 5) the civic aim, which prepares learners for public life."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: treating education and schooling as identical, which hides all the learning that happens outside classrooms. Mistake: assuming schools have a single purpose, when in fact they juggle several that can compete. Mistake: confusing an aim with a method - critical thinking is a purpose, while group discussion is one method that might serve it. Exit: (a) Name two purposes of education and give an example of a tension between them. (b) True or false: all education is schooling. Answers: for example personal growth and economic preparation, which compete when a timetable is narrowed to job training; false, because much education is informal or non-formal and never happens inside a school at all."}]}'::jsonb, 'Keep education and schooling apart: education is the broad, lifelong process of learning, while schooling is the formal institution we build to deliver part of it.', 12),
  (3, 'A brief history of education and major reformers', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Sketch how education changed from ancient and religious instruction to mass public schooling, and name several reformers who shaped today''s classrooms. Warm-up: before the printing press, why was memorization so central to learning? By the end you will be able to place the common school, compulsory attendance, and child-centered methods on a rough timeline, and connect each reformer to the idea they are remembered for. This history explains why schools look the way they do. Age-graded classes, fixed timetables, and report cards are inventions, not laws of nature, and knowing where they came from helps you question which ones still serve learning and which are simply habit."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"For most of history, formal education was for a few. Ancient scribal schools trained record-keepers; Greek and Roman tutors served elites; medieval learning lived mainly in religious institutions, where reading often meant reading scripture. The printing press, in the fifteenth century, spread texts and literacy widely. The Reformation pushed the idea that ordinary people should read for themselves. By the seventeenth century, reformers argued for teaching everyone. Industrial societies in the nineteenth century built mass public systems: tax-funded common schools, trained teachers, age-graded classrooms, and eventually compulsory attendance laws. The twentieth century expanded access further, first to secondary school for all and then to wider higher education, while debating how to teach and swinging between rote transmission and active, child-centered methods. The long story is a slow move from education as a privilege for the few toward education as a right for all, together with an ongoing argument about whether schooling should mainly transmit fixed knowledge or develop the whole learner."},{"heading":"Key thinkers & examples","text":"Comenius, a seventeenth-century reformer from Moravia, argued for universal education and orderly, picture-based textbooks, earning the title father of modern education. Jean-Jacques Rousseau, in Emile, romanticized learning through nature and the child''s own development. Johann Pestalozzi and Friedrich Froebel turned those ideas into method, and Froebel invented the kindergarten, a garden for children. Horace Mann championed the tax-funded common school in the United States and the professional training of teachers. Later, John Dewey made the school a site of democratic, experience-based learning. Paulo Freire, in Brazil, attacked what he called the banking model, in which teachers simply deposit facts into passive students, and argued instead for dialogue that frees learners. Example: the familiar kindergarten, report card, and age-graded class each came from a specific reformer or era, not from nowhere."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Which invention greatly accelerated literacy in the fifteenth century? 2) What is the common school, and who promoted it in the United States? 3) Who invented the kindergarten? 4) What did Freire call the model where teachers simply deposit facts? 5) Name one feature of modern classrooms that is a historical invention rather than a natural fact. Answers: 1) the printing press, which made texts cheap and widespread. 2) a tax-funded school open to all children; Horace Mann promoted it. 3) Friedrich Froebel. 4) the banking model of education. 5) age-graded classes, fixed timetables, or report cards are all examples of invented features."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: assuming schools have always looked like today''s, when graded classes and compulsory attendance are quite recent. Mistake: crediting a single hero for all progress, when reform came from many hands over centuries. Mistake: reading the history as pure, steady progress, since methods swing back and forth and old practices such as recitation survive alongside new ones. Exit: (a) Put these in order from earliest to latest: compulsory attendance laws, medieval religious schooling, the printing press. (b) Match Comenius and Mann to one idea each. Answers: medieval religious schooling, then the printing press, then compulsory attendance laws; Comenius for universal, orderly, illustrated instruction, and Mann for the tax-funded common school and teacher training."}]}'::jsonb, 'History moves from memorized recitation toward active, learner-centered methods, but older methods never fully vanish - most real classrooms blend both.', 13),
  (4, 'Philosophies of education', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Distinguish four major philosophies of education - perennialism, essentialism, progressivism, and social reconstructionism - and recognize each in real classroom choices. Warm-up: should a curriculum center on timeless great books, on useful core skills, on the child''s interests, or on changing society? Each answer names a philosophy. By the end you will be able to state what each philosophy treats as most worth knowing, place them on a spectrum from teacher-centered to learner-centered, and explain why a single teacher might borrow from several at once. This helps you read debates about school reform, which are usually clashes between these underlying philosophies even when no one names them out loud."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"A philosophy of education is a reasoned stance on what is worth teaching and how. Perennialism holds that certain truths and great works are timeless, so students should study enduring ideas through classic texts and disciplined reasoning; it is teacher-centered and intellectual. Essentialism also values a common core, but a practical one: the essential knowledge and skills - reading, writing, mathematics, science, history - that every citizen needs, delivered through structured, rigorous instruction. Progressivism, rooted in Dewey, centers the learner: start from children''s interests and experience, learn by doing, and treat the classroom as a small democracy. Social reconstructionism goes further, arguing that schools should actively address injustice and prepare students to reform society. The four sit roughly on a spectrum: perennialism and essentialism emphasize fixed, teacher-led content, while progressivism and reconstructionism emphasize active learners and change. Most real teaching blends them; a teacher may run a rigorous essentialist mathematics block and a progressive project in science within the same week."},{"heading":"Key thinkers & examples","text":"Perennialism is linked to Robert Hutchins and Mortimer Adler, whose Great Books program and Paideia Proposal pushed a shared classical curriculum. Essentialism is associated with William Bagley, who defended a firm common core against what he saw as soft, interest-driven schooling. Progressivism is embodied by John Dewey and his laboratory school, where children learned through purposeful activity. Social reconstructionism is tied to George Counts, who asked whether schools dare build a new social order, and connects to Paulo Freire''s liberating pedagogy. Example in practice: deciding whether ninth graders read Homer because it is a timeless classic (perennialism), read nonfiction to build strong literacy for any job (essentialism), choose their own novels to spark engagement (progressivism), or study texts on justice to examine their own community (social reconstructionism)."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Which philosophy centers on timeless great works and universal truths? 2) Which stresses a practical common core of essential skills? 3) Which starts from the learner''s interests and learning by doing? 4) Which asks schools to help reform society? 5) On a teacher-centered to learner-centered spectrum, where do perennialism and progressivism fall? Answers: 1) perennialism. 2) essentialism. 3) progressivism. 4) social reconstructionism. 5) perennialism is firmly teacher-centered, while progressivism is learner-centered, with essentialism and reconstructionism falling between."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: treating these as rival teams you must join, when most teachers mix them deliberately. Mistake: confusing perennialism and essentialism; both value a core, but perennialism prizes timeless ideas while essentialism prizes useful basics. Mistake: assuming progressivism means no structure or no content, when it really means organizing content around active, meaningful experience. Exit: (a) Name the philosophy behind the claim that schools should prepare students to challenge injustice. (b) Give one classroom decision that would look different under essentialism versus progressivism. Answers: social reconstructionism; for example, a fixed common reading list and direct instruction under essentialism versus student-chosen projects built on interests under progressivism."}]}'::jsonb, 'A philosophy of education answers two questions at once - what is worth teaching, and how it should be taught. Keep the two apart when you compare them.', 13),
  (5, 'Learning theories for teaching', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Explain the three big families of learning theory - behaviorism, cognitivism, and constructivism - and connect them to Dewey, Piaget, Vygotsky, and Montessori. Warm-up: when a student finally understands long division, did they absorb a rewarded response, process new information, or build the idea for themselves? Each answer points to a different theory. By the end you will be able to match a classroom technique to the theory behind it, and summarize what each of the four named figures contributed. Theories are not abstract trivia here; the method you reach for in a given moment usually rests on a quiet assumption about how learning works, and naming that assumption makes you a sharper teacher."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Behaviorism, from Pavlov, Watson, and Skinner, treats learning as a change in observable behavior shaped by stimulus, response, and reinforcement; praise, practice, and clear feedback are its tools, and it works well for facts and skills that need fluency. Cognitivism opens up the mind as an information processor: attention, memory, and mental schemas matter, so teaching manages cognitive load, organizes material, and builds on prior knowledge. Constructivism holds that learners actively build understanding rather than receive it; knowledge is constructed through experience and reflection. These are not simply right or wrong - each explains part of learning and suits different goals. Memorizing multiplication facts leans behaviorist; grasping why a proof works leans cognitivist and constructivist. Skilled teachers move between them: drill where fluency is needed, structured explanation where new concepts are dense, and hands-on inquiry where deep understanding is the goal. The field broadly shifted from behaviorist to cognitive to constructivist emphases across the twentieth century."},{"heading":"Key thinkers & examples","text":"John Dewey, a pragmatist and early constructivist, argued that we learn by doing and reflecting on experience. Jean Piaget mapped stages of cognitive development, showing children think differently at different ages and construct schemas by assimilating and accommodating new experiences. Lev Vygotsky added the social side: learning is mediated by language and culture, and the zone of proximal development is the gap between what a learner can do alone and with guidance, which is the home of scaffolding. Maria Montessori designed prepared environments with self-correcting materials, letting children learn through independent, hands-on activity at their own pace. Example: a teacher draws on Vygotsky when pairing a struggling reader with a stronger peer, and on Piaget when choosing concrete materials because the class is not yet ready for abstract symbols."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Which theory explains learning as reinforced behavior? 2) Whose stages describe how children''s thinking changes with age? 3) What does Vygotsky''s zone of proximal development describe? 4) Which figure designed prepared environments with self-correcting materials? 5) Name a classroom task well suited to behaviorist methods. Answers: 1) behaviorism. 2) Jean Piaget. 3) the gap between what a learner can do alone and what they can do with guidance. 4) Maria Montessori. 5) drilling arithmetic facts or spelling for fluency, with immediate feedback, fits behaviorist methods well."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: declaring one theory the only true one, when each illuminates different parts of learning. Mistake: confusing Piaget and Vygotsky - Piaget stresses individual developmental stages, while Vygotsky stresses social and cultural mediation. Mistake: equating constructivism with no teaching, when it actually demands careful task design and skilled facilitation. Exit: (a) Match scaffolding and reinforcement to their theories. (b) Why might a teacher use concrete objects before abstract symbols? Answers: scaffolding belongs to Vygotsky''s social constructivism and reinforcement to behaviorism; because, following Piaget, learners often grasp concrete representations before they can reason well with abstract symbols."}]}'::jsonb, 'A learning theory is a claim about how learning happens; a teaching method is what you do about it. One theory can support several different methods.', 14),
  (6, 'Checkpoint 1', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"The main purpose most commonly attributed to formal education includes transmitting knowledge, skills, and...","options":["only sports","values and cultural norms","tax revenue","military ranks"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The cultural aim passes on language, values, and shared knowledge across generations."},{"prompt":"Which reformer is most associated with the tax-funded common school in the United States?","options":["Horace Mann","Isaac Newton","Adam Smith","Carl Linnaeus"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Horace Mann championed the common school and professional teacher training."},{"prompt":"Essentialism as a philosophy emphasizes...","options":["abandoning any fixed curriculum","a core of essential knowledge and skills for all","learning only through free play","no assessment at all"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Essentialism defends a practical common core delivered through structured, rigorous instruction."},{"prompt":"Vygotsky''s zone of proximal development refers to...","options":["the distance to the nearest school","what a learner can do with guidance but not yet alone","the exam hall","the recess yard"],"answer":1,"explanation":"It is the gap between independent performance and what is possible with support - the home of scaffolding."},{"prompt":"Behaviorism explains learning primarily through...","options":["unconscious dreams","stimulus, response, and reinforcement","genetics alone","random chance"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Behaviorism treats learning as observable behavior shaped by reinforcement and practice."},{"prompt":"Maria Montessori is best known for...","options":["prepared environments and child-led, hands-on learning","standardized multiple-choice testing","military drill","abolishing all schools"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Montessori designed prepared environments with self-correcting materials for independent learning."}]}'::jsonb, null, 8),
  (7, 'Curriculum, instructional design, and assessment', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Explain the basics of curriculum and instructional design, and tell formative from summative assessment while using Bloom''s taxonomy to aim your objectives. Warm-up: if your goal is that students can analyze a poem, is a multiple-choice recall quiz a good measure of it? By the end you will be able to write a clear learning objective, choose an assessment that matches it, and place a task on Bloom''s ladder from remembering up to creating. These are the everyday tools of planning. Most lessons that fall flat do so because the objective was vague, or because the test quietly measured something different from what the teaching aimed at."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Curriculum is the planned content and sequence of what students should learn; instructional design is how you arrange activities so they actually learn it. A widely used approach is backward design: first name the desired outcome, then decide what evidence would show it, and only then plan the lessons. Objectives should be specific and observable - students will be able to compare two sources beats students will understand sources. Assessment comes in two broad types. Formative assessment happens during learning, through quick checks, drafts, and questioning, and its purpose is to guide the next teaching move; it usually is not graded heavily. Summative assessment happens at the end - a final exam, project, or essay - and measures what was learned. Bloom''s taxonomy gives a ladder of thinking: remember, understand, apply, analyze, evaluate, create. It helps you aim objectives and match assessments, since a recall quiz tests the bottom rungs while an original design tests the top. Alignment is the key idea: objective, instruction, and assessment should all point at the same target."},{"heading":"Key thinkers & examples","text":"Ralph Tyler framed curriculum around four questions: what purposes, what experiences, how to organize them, and how to evaluate the result. Benjamin Bloom and colleagues created the taxonomy of educational objectives in 1956; Anderson and Krathwohl revised it in 2001, turning the nouns into verbs and placing create at the top. Grant Wiggins and Jay McTighe popularized backward design in Understanding by Design. Example of alignment: if the objective is to analyze the causes of a historical event, which is an analyze-level goal, a matching summative task is a short essay weighing those causes, not a true-or-false recall quiz. A matching formative check might be an exit ticket asking students to list and rank two causes, letting you spot confusion before the essay is due."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) What is backward design, in one sentence? 2) Give the difference between formative and summative assessment. 3) Put these Bloom levels in order from lowest to highest: analyze, remember, create. 4) Is a draft reviewed mid-project formative or summative? 5) Rewrite ''students will know photosynthesis'' as an observable objective. Answers: 1) decide the outcome and its evidence first, then plan instruction to reach it. 2) formative guides learning in progress and is low-stakes, while summative measures learning at the end. 3) remember, analyze, create. 4) formative. 5) for example, students will be able to explain how plants convert light into chemical energy and diagram the inputs and outputs."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: writing vague objectives like understand or appreciate that cannot be observed or measured. Mistake: misaligning assessment and goal, such as testing recall when you taught analysis. Mistake: treating every quiz as summative, when much assessment should be formative and ungraded. Exit: (a) Name the top level of the revised Bloom''s taxonomy. (b) Your objective is that students can evaluate an argument; name one matching assessment. Answers: create; for example a short critique that judges the argument against stated criteria, rather than a fact-recall quiz that never asks for judgment at all."}]}'::jsonb, 'Plan backward: decide the goal and how you will measure it before you design the activities. Teach to the objective, not to the worksheet.', 13),
  (8, 'Key terms', 'vocab', '{"items":[{"term":"pedagogy","translation":"the art and science of teaching: how instruction is designed and delivered","example":"Her pedagogy mixed direct instruction with hands-on projects."},{"term":"curriculum","translation":"the planned content, skills, and sequence a course sets out to teach","example":"The new science curriculum adds a unit on climate."},{"term":"scaffolding","translation":"temporary support that lets a learner do a task they could not yet manage alone, removed as skill grows","example":"Sentence starters are scaffolding for young writers."},{"term":"formative assessment","translation":"low-stakes checks during learning used to guide the next teaching step","example":"An exit ticket is a quick formative assessment."},{"term":"summative assessment","translation":"an end-of-unit measure of what was learned, such as a final exam or project","example":"The final essay served as the summative assessment."},{"term":"differentiation","translation":"adjusting content, process, or product so varied learners can all reach the goal","example":"Offering a choice of reading levels is differentiation."},{"term":"zone of proximal development","translation":"the gap between what a learner can do alone and what they can do with guidance","example":"Vygotsky located real learning in the zone of proximal development."},{"term":"metacognition","translation":"thinking about one''s own thinking and learning, such as planning and self-checking","example":"Asking students to explain their strategy builds metacognition."}]}'::jsonb, null, 9),
  (9, 'Classroom management, motivation, equity, and inclusion', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Set up routines and relationships that keep learning on track, use what we know about motivation, and design lessons that include every learner. Warm-up: does a well-managed classroom mean a silent one? By the end you will be able to name proactive management strategies, distinguish intrinsic from extrinsic motivation, and explain how equity, inclusion, and differentiation widen access for diverse students. These are the human skills that make the rest of teaching possible. A brilliant lesson plan fails in a chaotic room, and a motivated, included class learns from an ordinary one, so this is where planning meets daily reality."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Classroom management is the set of practices that create a safe, orderly, and productive environment. The most effective management is proactive: clear expectations, consistent routines, engaging tasks, and strong relationships prevent most problems before they start, which beats reacting with punishment. Motivation drives effort. Extrinsic motivation comes from outside rewards and consequences such as grades and praise, while intrinsic motivation comes from genuine interest, purpose, and the satisfaction of mastery. Self-determination theory says motivation grows when students feel competence, autonomy, and relatedness, so good teachers offer appropriate challenge, meaningful choice, and belonging. Equity means giving each student what they need to succeed, which is not the same as treating everyone identically. Inclusion means teaching students of differing abilities, languages, and backgrounds together, with supports. Differentiation is the practical craft of adjusting content, process, or product so varied learners can all reach the goal. Together these ideas reframe management from controlling behavior toward designing conditions in which diverse learners want to engage and are able to."},{"heading":"Key thinkers & examples","text":"Jacob Kounin found that effective managers show withitness, an awareness of everything happening at once, and keep lessons moving, preventing disruption rather than merely punishing it. Carol Dweck''s work on mindset shows that praising effort and strategy, rather than fixed ability, builds persistence. Edward Deci and Richard Ryan''s self-determination theory explains intrinsic motivation through competence, autonomy, and relatedness. Universal Design for Learning, developed by the CAST organization, urges planning multiple means of engagement, representation, and expression from the start so fewer students need retrofitted accommodations. Example: instead of only lecturing, a teacher offers a reading, a diagram, and a short video for representation, lets students show mastery by essay or presentation for expression, and ties the topic to student interests for engagement - one inclusive design serving many learners at once."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Is proactive or reactive management more effective, and why? 2) Give an example of intrinsic and of extrinsic motivation. 3) What three needs does self-determination theory name? 4) How does equity differ from treating everyone identically? 5) Name one way to differentiate a lesson. Answers: 1) proactive, because clear routines and engaging tasks prevent problems that punishment only reacts to. 2) intrinsic: reading for the joy of it; extrinsic: reading to earn a grade or sticker. 3) competence, autonomy, and relatedness. 4) equity gives each learner what they specifically need, rather than the same thing to all. 5) adjust the content, the process, or the product - for example offer a choice of how to show mastery."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: equating a good classroom with silence, when productive noise during collaboration is perfectly fine. Mistake: relying only on rewards and punishments, which can erode intrinsic motivation over time. Mistake: confusing equality and equity, or treating inclusion as a favor rather than a right and a design goal. Exit: (a) Name the three needs in self-determination theory. (b) Give one Universal Design for Learning move that includes more learners. Answers: competence, autonomy, and relatedness; offering multiple means of representation, such as text plus a diagram plus a video, so different learners can reach the same content through the path that works for them."}]}'::jsonb, 'Management is not control for its own sake; it protects learning time. Pair clear structure with warmth, and design for the full range of learners.', 13),
  (10, 'Education systems and the teaching profession', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Describe how education systems are structured, funded, and governed, and explain what it means to be a reflective professional. Warm-up: who decides what your school teaches and how much money it gets - the teacher, the school, the district, or the national government? Often all of them share the job. By the end you will be able to outline the levels of an education system, name common funding sources, and describe a reflective practice cycle you can use to improve your own teaching. This closes the course by connecting the big picture, how systems are run, to the personal one, how an individual teacher grows over a career through deliberate reflection rather than mere repetition."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Education systems are usually organized in levels - early childhood, primary, secondary, and tertiary - with governance shared among national, regional or state, and local authorities. Some countries run centralized systems with a national curriculum and exams; others, like the United States, push many decisions down to states and districts, producing wide local variation. Funding typically mixes government revenue, often from taxes, and sometimes fees or private sources; because local taxes vary, funding can be unequal, which sits at the center of many policy and equity debates. Policy sets standards, accountability measures, teacher qualifications, and how results are reported. Within this system sits the teaching profession: teachers are not mere deliverers of a script but professionals who plan, assess, and adapt. Reflective practice, described by Donald Schon as reflection in and on action, is the habit of examining your teaching, gathering evidence of its effects, and adjusting. It is what turns years of experience into genuine expertise, rather than the same year repeated many times over."},{"heading":"Key thinkers & examples","text":"Donald Schon introduced the reflective practitioner, distinguishing reflection-in-action, which is thinking on your feet mid-lesson, from reflection-on-action, which is reviewing afterward. David Kolb''s experiential learning cycle - concrete experience, reflective observation, abstract conceptualization, and active experimentation - gives teachers a loop for improvement. Graham Gibbs turned reflection into practical steps: describe, feel, evaluate, analyze, conclude, and plan. Example of system and profession meeting: a national policy raises a mathematics standard; a district funds coaching; and an individual teacher, using a reflective cycle, tries a new approach, examines student work, and refines the lesson for next time. Comparative cases such as Finland, often cited for strong teacher preparation and high professional trust, show how policy choices about the profession shape daily classroom life."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Name the four common levels of an education system. 2) Give one reason school funding can be unequal between places. 3) What is the difference between reflection-in-action and reflection-on-action? 4) Name two stages of a reflective cycle. 5) Who popularized the idea of the reflective practitioner? Answers: 1) early childhood, primary, secondary, and tertiary. 2) reliance on local taxes, which vary with local wealth. 3) reflection-in-action happens during teaching, while reflection-on-action happens afterward when reviewing. 4) for example describe and plan from Gibbs, or concrete experience and active experimentation from Kolb. 5) Donald Schon."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: assuming every country runs schooling the same way, when systems range from highly centralized to highly local. Mistake: treating reflection as vague worrying rather than a structured cycle tied to evidence of student learning. Mistake: thinking funding is neutral, when how money is raised and shared is itself a major equity decision. Exit: (a) Name one centralized and one decentralized feature a system might have. (b) Give the steps of any reflective cycle you know. Answers: for example a national curriculum is centralized while local district budgets are decentralized; for instance the Gibbs cycle runs describe, feel, evaluate, analyze, conclude, and plan."}]}'::jsonb, 'Policy and funding set the conditions you teach in; reflective practice is how you keep improving within them. Know both the system and your own craft.', 14)
) as v(pos, title, kind, body, notes, mins)
